From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 23:22:31 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] ARM64EC: MADEIRA_FEX_PAUSE=yield-os[:K], a PAUSE that gives
 the core back

Apple's cores treat YIELD, what FEX compiles x86 PAUSE to, as a NOP,
so a guest spin-wait holds its core at full rate; WFE (fex 0083) holds
it too, as a thread waiting in it still counts as running, which cost
The Witcher 3 five times the frames over 150 ms (decision 0163). Under
the power clamp the P cores park, the four E cores are all busy, and
the game's render thread, spinning on PAUSE and QueryPerformanceCounter
at witcher3.exe+0x1c69ae0, is the busiest of them.

MADEIRA_FEX_PAUSE=yield-os[:K] keeps PAUSE a YIELD, counts each one in
the guest thread's frame (CpuStateFrame::IosPauseCount) and makes every
K-th one (default 128, 1 to 4095) a swtch_pri Mach trap, what Darwin's
sched_yield is: the kernel depresses the thread and runs another
runnable thread on the core if one waits, else returns at once
(0.3 us on an M1 Pro). The trap is raw, svc #0x80 with x16 = -59, so no
Wine syscall frame or register spill is involved; Mach traps keep every
register but x0, and the sequence saves and restores x0 and x16 and
uses SUB and CBZ, not CMP, so the guest's NZCV is untouched.

The sequence is in JIT/IosPauseYieldOs.h and the option's parser in
FEXCore/Utils/IosPauseOption.h, so a host test can compile both;
"FEX: pause mode=" now logs after=K. MADEIRA_FEX_PAUSE is already
keyed by the disk cache (IosCodegenSwitches.h), K included.

Linear: PLA-462
Class: feature
Evidence: tools/tests/test_fex_pause_yield_os.py
Offered-upstream: no; the switch is Playport's iOS host's
